<h2>Installing and Using Skills (Hands-on)</h2>
<p>Practicing with skills is almost disappointingly simple &mdash; <strong>"install this skill"</strong> is all it takes. That said, it is far more interesting when you keep the <strong>mechanics</strong> you learned earlier in mind as you use them.</p>

<h3>Installing</h3>
<ul>
    <li>Pressing the <strong>"Get this skill"</strong> button on a skill's distribution page copies its git address.</li>
    <li>Paste that address into Claude Code and say <strong>"install this skill"</strong> and it gets installed. (For a manual install, copy it into the corresponding folder.)</li>
    <li>By default it is installed in the <strong>global folder</strong>. (You can also choose project or global, but you need not worry about it at first.)</li>
    <li>If it does not show up after installing, run <code>/reload</code> (reload plugins) or <strong>quit and restart Claude Code</strong> and it will pick the skill up. (<code>/clear</code> is different &mdash; it only empties the context.)</li>
</ul>
<p>On a smartphone you can also try skills out with the <strong>Claude app</strong> (which runs in a sandbox).</p>

<h3>Using</h3>
<pre><code><span class="c"># Check the list of skills available to you</span>
What skills do I have available?
<span class="c"># Explicit invocation</span>
/skill-name &lt;command&gt;</code></pre>

<h3>Hands-on &mdash; an organization brand skill</h3>
<p>Install a <strong>brand guideline skill</strong> built from the style of an institution's website, then make a request like this.</p>
<pre><code>Using the &lt;brand&gt; brand guideline skill,
make a summer workshop page</code></pre>
<p>The page then comes out <strong>in the organization's brand style</strong> rather than any old way. The point is not prettiness &mdash; it is that <strong>everyone using the same skill gets a similar style</strong>, producing <strong>consistent, reusable</strong> deliverables across the organization. In the same way you use a <strong>PPT template skill</strong> (presentation decks), a <strong>Finder skill</strong> (finding experts in the organization), and so on.</p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Triggering recap</span>
    The reason a skill fires even when you just say "make it in the organization's style" is that the <strong>metadata description</strong> states "when using this style, use this skill." Still, to be certain it is better to <strong>name the skill explicitly</strong>.
</div>
